  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nA low amplitude trough across the Great Lakes and New England \nis providing west northwest flow across the Upper Midwest. Some \nmid/high level clouds are streaming overhead this afternoon. \nDespite the clouds, temperatures have responded with early \nafternoon readings in the 60s. We should gain another few \ndegrees over the next few hours, especially if enough sun breaks\nout from the clouds, and some low 70s are likely in spots. \nClouds will clear out tonight and early Friday.\n\nA substantial warming trend will take place this weekend as the\ntrough lifts out and strong ridging builds across the central\nand eastern U.S. Surface flow will remain southeast Friday,\nwhich will limit the degree of warm air advection. Still, highs\nin the 70s are expected. A broad area of low pressure will\ndevelop across the northern Plains Saturday. Southerly flow \nwill strengthen and impressive warm air advection will begin \nlate Friday night. Low temperatures will occur Friday evening,\nthen rise through the 60s overnight, possibly into the low 70s\nby sunrise Saturday. Winds may gust to 40 mph on the Buffalo \nRidge toward morning, then those gusts should overspread the \nregion by late morning as mixing deepens. With full sun all day \nand deep mixing expected, temperatures will easily reach their \nfull potential. 925 mb temps of +23 to +25C suggest highs in the\nmid to upper 80s. As such the forecast has been increased, but \nnot quite to the NBM 90th percentile yet. The 90th percentile \nhas not been warm enough during the past few days, so there may \nbe some upward adjustments yet. It will be interesting to see \nif we can break 90 one more time this season. The record\nSaturday is 90 at MSP, which is also the latest 90 on record.\nLow temperatures are also in jeopardy with 62 forecast Friday\nevening before rising late and 68 Saturday night. Records for\nboth nights are 63. The cold front will hold off until later\nMonday, so the very warm conditions will persist through at\nleast Sunday night. Highs in the 80s are again expected Sunday.\nModel spread increases significantly Monday due to the timing\ndifferences with the front. Best chances for precip will remain\nnorth, with the frontal passage trending drier across southern \nMN and WI.\n\nThe very warm airmass brings elevated fire weather concerns. \nDeep mixing and very warm temperatures will likely allow RH to \ndrop at least into the 30s by afternoon. If they drop into the\nlow to mid 20s, critical fire weather conditions will occur.\nThis type of pattern in October and November usually leads to \nan overprediction of dew points with RH verifying lower than \npredicted, particularly across western MN. \n\nFollowing the front Monday, cooler air will settle in across the\nnorthern CONUS through at least midweek. By Wednesday, a trough\nover the Rockies will spawn and area of low pressure over the \nwestern High Plains, then tracks it northeast late week. There\nremains quite a bit of spread in this quickly evolving pattern,\nbut generally precip chances will increase Wednesday and \nThursday. Depending on the track of the system, it may be warmer\nor cooler than upper 50s to mid 60s as is currently forecast.",
